.TH STYXTEST 1
.SH NAME
styxtest \- check that a trace is read as the right paths
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B styxtest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Styxtest
builds Styx traces with known contents, runs
.IR styxlog (1)
over them, and checks which paths it says were opened.
.PP
A trace is messages about numbered fids, and a walk is what gives a fid its
meaning.
Following those is the whole of what
.IR styxlog (1)
does, and the way to get it wrong is subtle enough to have happened.
.SH "WHAT IT CHECKS"
That a single walk names its fid; that two walks in flight at once keep their
own paths, whether their replies come back in order or not; that a walk which
stopped early or failed outright leaves the fid unnamed; and that a clunked fid
is forgotten, so a later fid of the same number does not inherit the old name.
.PP
And that a recorded program replays byte for byte.
That one needs a program to run, so it is an integration check rather than the
built-trace kind.
.PP
Its control is the important half.
If
.IR styxreplay (1)'s
mount quietly did nothing, the program would read the real file and the output
would match anyway \(em so the file is
.I removed
before the replay, and what comes out can then only have come from the trace.
.PP
It builds the traces rather than making them, because every interesting case
is about concurrency and none of them can be produced by tracing one program.
The trace is a documented format, so it is written directly \(em the same
reason
.IR sessiontest (1)
builds recordings.
.SH "THE BUG IT GUARDS"
A single slot for the walk in flight.
It is correct for one process and silently wrong for two, because their walks
interleave and one process's reply then takes the other's path.
The output stays entirely plausible, which is why nothing noticed until a
concurrent trace was looked at deliberately.
.PP
Putting that bug back makes exactly the two interleaved checks fail and leaves
the single-walk one passing, which is the shape the fault really had.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/styxtest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR styxlog (1),
.IR styxreplay (1),
.IR iostats (4),
.IR sessiontest (1)
.PP
.B /doc/session.ms
\(em
.IR "Recording and Replaying an Inferno Session" ,
a tutorial covering all of this and how it was checked.
.SH BUGS
It checks the reading of a trace, not the making of one.
That
.B iostats -t
writes what actually crossed is not tested here.
.PP
The replay check covers one program reading one file.
Divergence is not checked here, though it does work: replaying a trace against a
different command reports which walk differed and stops.
.PP
It reports through the same standard output it redirects while running
.IR styxlog (1),
restoring it afterwards.
If that redirection ever fails the results go into the captured file and the
run looks silent.
